A federal funds futures price is a traded rate, and that is how it works as a forecast

00:05:02.623 --> 00:05:05.583
of policy only after someone does extra arithmetic.

00:05:06.143 --> 00:05:09.683
The quote prices the average federal funds rate over a future month.

00:05:10.243 --> 00:05:14.763
Getting from there to the chance of a decision at one meeting means stripping a term premium,

00:05:15.263 --> 00:05:19.023
then fitting what is left onto the discrete choices the committee actually has.

00:05:19.443 --> 00:05:25.283
A 2016 note in the Federal Reserve's FEDS notes series studies those front end premiums

00:05:25.283 --> 00:05:30.203
with futures plus the desk's survey of primary dealers and its survey of market participants,

00:05:30.623 --> 00:05:35.123
and it treats the premium you subtract as an assumption, not as a figure the futures

00:05:35.123 --> 00:05:36.343
price states on its own.

00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:39.523
Take the futures implied rate for the month of the meeting.

00:05:40.123 --> 00:05:42.923
Subtract the rate you think holds if the committee stands still.

00:05:43.563 --> 00:05:47.623
The gap still mixes payment for bearing rate risk with the chance of a different setting.

00:05:48.323 --> 00:05:49.943
Subtract the premium you assumed.

00:05:50.523 --> 00:05:53.083
Divide the remainder by the size of the step on the menu.

00:05:53.083 --> 00:05:57.583
you. Swap the premium and the probability moves while the futures price sits still.

00:05:58.243 --> 00:06:03.543
A 2009 paper in the Journal of Money, Credit and Banking says many premium estimates assume

00:06:03.543 --> 00:06:10.023
average forecast errors are zero, or that survey stand in for expectations. An unanticipated

00:06:10.023 --> 00:06:15.183
slide in the funds rate breaks the first assumption. Strategic answers from survey respondents

00:06:15.183 --> 00:06:20.603
break the second. Premiums built that way can be biased. Their alternative method finds

00:06:20.603 --> 00:06:26.143
premiums often negative since 2000, generally in line with the negative betas of that decade.

00:06:26.663 --> 00:06:30.323
A negative premium puts the futures rate below the expected future rate,

00:06:30.703 --> 00:06:35.143
so treating the raw quote as the expectation understates the chance of higher settings.

